# choose-agent

A dispatch primitive. Run the script; read its one-line JSON result.

```bash
scripts/choose-agent [--only <name|kind>]... [--exclude <name|kind>]... [--prefer <name|kind>]...
scripts/choose-agent --check [--only <name|kind>]... [--exclude <name|kind>]...
```

Load once per session; takes no `args`. Run the script directly via Bash for each launch — don't re-invoke this skill per item. It needs no Herdr socket, so it runs standalone.

## Constraints are yours to gather

The script can't read the conversation or the repository, so turn what they say into flags before running it, most authoritative first:

1. What the user said in this session.
2. A pin on this piece of work, such as a unit's recorded kind.
3. The repository's instructions (`AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`), such as "implement with Codex" or "never use local models here".
4. What you know the work needs. If a worktree's sandbox settings exist only for one kind, the other kind can't commit there, so exclude it.

A requirement is `--only` or `--exclude`; a leaning is `--prefer`, which tilts the odds without overriding usage. When sources conflict, the higher one wins, and you pass only the result. Pass nothing when nothing applies; the machine's candidates and usage then decide alone.

## What it does

- Reads the machine's candidates from `~/.config/choose-agent/candidates.toml`, or uses Claude and Codex at 40/60 when the file is absent. Each `[[candidate]]` has a `kind` (an agent kind `launch-agent-in-pane` accepts), and optionally a `name` (defaults to the kind), a `model` hint, a `quota` (`claude`, `chatgpt`, or `none` for an unmetered local model; the default is `none`), and a `weight` (default 1).
- Drops candidates ruled out by `--only` or `--exclude`, candidates whose CLI isn't on PATH, and metered candidates not signed in to their quota.
- Weighs each metered candidate by its usage, from `ai-usage --json windows`. Capacity about to reset unused raises its weight, a nearly spent window lowers it, and a window at 90% rules it out. An unmetered candidate, or one whose usage couldn't be read, keeps its base weight, so a failed usage fetch never blocks a launch.
- Picks at random by weight rather than taking the top score, so a burst of launches against one usage snapshot spreads out.
- Raises backpressure when every candidate the work could go to has a usage window ahead of pace: a larger share used than 90% of the share of the window gone, the even pace that reaches the 90% cutoff exactly at the reset, so an agent keeping to it is never ruled out. A 5-hour session at 21% with four hours left is ahead; a week at 80% with an hour left is not. While any candidate is on pace, the weights already steer around the others, so there is nothing to report. The constraints count: with `--only claude`, Claude's usage alone decides.

## Output (stdout, JSON)

`{ name, kind, model, reason, backpressure, candidates_from, candidates, dropped }`

Launch with `kind` and, when it is not null, `model` as the model hint. Record `name` wherever the work's kind is recorded, and put `reason` in any log of the launch, since it says which constraint or usage figure decided.

`backpressure` is null, or `{ message, until, windows }`: the message reads "Approaching usage quota: recommend stopping work until <until>.", followed by a note that it is advice only, where `until` is the earliest time one candidate has all its windows back on pace (assuming no further use, so usually before the reset), and `windows` lists the ones that raised it. `until` is pushed later by a random few percent of that window's length, so agents following the same snapshot don't all resume at once and push it straight back ahead of pace; it is null when a window reports no reset time. It is advice only. Ignore it by default and launch as usual; act on it only when the user, your instructions, or a workflow you are following explicitly asks you to follow usage backpressure, and then as they say.

## Checking without launching

`--check` reports backpressure without choosing a candidate, for an agent that runs long enough to never pass through a launch again. It takes the same `--only` and `--exclude` and prints `{ backpressure, candidates_from, candidates, dropped }`. A running agent checks its own quota with `--only <its name or kind>`, between tasks rather than in the middle of one; a dispatcher checks its pool with the flags it would launch with. Every candidate being at its usage limit is not needs-judgment here, because that is exactly what the backpressure reports; only an empty set of candidates exits `2`. The result is advice in the same way, so it means something only to an agent that was asked to follow it.

## Exit codes

- `0` — success, result on stdout.
- `2` — needs judgment: nothing is available under the constraints, or every remaining candidate is at its usage limit. stdout is `{"status": "needs_judgment", "reason": ...}` with the candidates considered and why each other one dropped, plus `backpressure` when the cause is usage, so its `until` says when work could resume. Take it to the user rather than loosening a constraint yourself; a requirement from them or the repository isn't yours to relax.
- `1` — real error (an unreadable candidates file). Diagnostics on stderr.
